.TH SHEDIT 1 "2026-11-16" "shedit 1.0" "Terminal text input"
.SH NAME
shedit \- terminal text input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shedit
.RB [ \-bghowx ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR rows ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR text ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shedit
is a terminal text input program. Unlike an editor it is not using the whole
terminal. It opens at the cursor position and waits for text input.
.PP
On submit the whole text is printed to stdout.
.PP
On start a text can be preloaded from stdin. See
.BR EXAMPLES .
.PP
.B shedit
is meant to be a simple input program. There is only minimal editing with
backspace and cursor keys. But it is possible to call your editor from
.BR $EDITOR .
.PP
.B shedit
is part of the
.B shui\-tools
collection.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Display the bottom bar in reverse video.
.TP
.B \-g
Disable the bottom bar.
.TP
.B \-h
Show a short usage message.
.TP
.B \-o
Turns
.B shedit
into a one line input.
Enter is submit and all keys for editor or newline are disabled.
\er\en are removed from stdin input.
.TP
.BI \-p " text"
Set the bottom bar text.
.TP
.BI \-r " lines"
Defines the height of the input field.
0 uses the whole terminal. 3 is default.
.TP
.B \-w
Enables line wrap.
.TP
.B \-x
Default CTRL\-D or ESC d are the submit keys.
With
.B \-x
Enter is a submit key.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B CTRL\-J
Insert a newline.
.TP
.BR CTRL\-E " or " "ESC e"
Open the editor set up in
.BR $EDITOR .
.TP
.BR CTRL\-X " or " ESC " or " "ESC q" " or " "ESC x"
Aborts without saving the text with return code 1.
.TP
.BR CTRL\-D " or " "ESC d"
Submit the text with return code 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Wait for a message to be typed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
chat=$(shedit)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Preload intro.txt with a height of 10 lines:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
chat=$(shedit \-r10 <intro.txt)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Edit a stream before saving:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
jq '.[]' file.json | shedit \-r 20 >new.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Edit a filename:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
new_name=$(printf "filename.txt" | shedit \-o)
.EE
.RE
